Changelog: 13-10-2026 00:34:26

Release 0.1.2.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 00:28:25

The PS4's connection history is named by its menu: Settings > Remote Play Connection Settings > Connection History, whose OPTIONS button clears all entries at once (the owner's PS4 Pro); clearing it keeps the registration, as Test session showed right after, in the hint under Registered consoles and the manual.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 00:25:00

The texts name the PS4's menus next to the PS5's: the PIN under Add Device (Remote Play Connection Settings), the error messages, the PIN field, the hint under Registered consoles, the uninstaller's goodbye and the manual. A PS4 lists no registered devices; its Remote Play menu keeps a connection history that can be cleared (seen on the owner's PS4 Pro: date, account, Nearby, device PC), so removing the board on the console is a PS5 step only.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 00:22:31

The app has its real icons (images/bc250-remote-play.png and -128.png, the owner's), in place of the placeholder.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 00:04:22

The session ID message is read as chiaki-ng's code implies: the first byte is the ID's length (0x4a, 74, for a normal ID), not a fixed marker; a length that does not match is noted. The owner's PS4 Pro sent the length 16 and the text InvalidSessionId (its bytes from the new log line): the console has no session ID to give, so the made-up one is used, as chiaki-ng does, and the log says so in plain words. That message is a test now (recorded bytes), and the fake console sends a 74-character ID as consoles do.

Changelog: 12-10-2026 23:58:32

First run on a real console (the owner's PS4, 12-10-2026): account ID through Sony's sign-in, registration, Test session and Test stream all worked (720p, 291 frames and 499 audio packets in 5 s; the console's key signature checks out). The PS4 sent a session ID message that starts with 10 instead of 4a, and the made-up session ID was used, as chiaki-ng does; the log line now shows that message's bytes (up to 48), to find out why.

Changelog: 12-10-2026 23:34:35

Find my account ID…: the PSN account ID through Sony's sign-in, as chiaki-ng does it (psn.py, from its scripts/psn-account-id.py and gui/src/psnaccountid.cpp). The browser opens Sony's sign-in page for the Remote Play client; the address Sony ends on ("redirect") is pasted back, and its one-time code is exchanged at Sony's token service for the account ID (user_id), which fills the account ID field. Only the account ID is kept, the token is dropped; the scope is chiaki-ng's narrowest (psn:clientapp). No new dependency (urllib) and no embedded browser. The registration hint names the PS4's menu (Remote Play Connection Settings > Add Device) next to the PS5's. Tests against a fake token service on localhost (tests/test_psn.py) and in the window test.

Changelog: 12-10-2026 23:09:42

native/Cargo.lock has the crate at 0.1.1, as Cargo.toml since the release; CI's cargo test --locked failed on the difference.

Changelog: 12-10-2026 22:58:48

Release 0.1.1.

Changelog: 12-10-2026 22:47:04

The stream connection, iteration 2 continued. The native core has the live Takion connection (native/src/connection.rs, Python class Takion): the handshake with retries, a receiving thread that checks every packet's GMAC, puts the data messages in order (reorder queue, reorder.rs) and acknowledges them, sends unacknowledged messages again (send buffer, sendbuffer.rs: every 200 ms, 25 times), holds video and audio until the keys are there, decrypts them and puts the video in order with chiaki-ng's one-frame wait for a missing packet. Ported from chiaki-ng's takion.c, takionsendbuffer.c and reorderqueue.c, and tested against a fake console on localhost in Rust. Differences: an acknowledgement that comes before the keys waits up to 100 ms for them, so the BANG's acknowledgement goes out signed as in chiaki-ng (there the handler runs before the acknowledgement); and dropping a queued data message empties its slot, where chiaki-ng's reorder queue leaves the freed entry in place. In Python: the stream's protobuf messages written and read by hand (tkproto.py), the launch spec (launchspec.py) and the stream connection (stream.py: BIG with the ECDH key, BANG and the stream keys, STREAMINFO with the video and audio headers, its acknowledgement, the controller and microphone messages, heartbeats, DISCONNECT), all equal to vectors from chiaki-ng's own code (tests/stream_vectors.json: launchspec.c, rpcrypt.c, and the messages encoded with nanopb as streamconnection.c builds them). The network test before the stream (Senkusha) is not there; the stream uses chiaki-ng's values for when it fails (MTU 1454, 1 ms). The console's signature on its key is checked and noted in the log, not required (chiaki-ng does not check it). New button Test stream: a session and its stream for 5 seconds (1080p60 from a PS5, 720p60 from a PS4), counting the frames and sound packets that arrive; the control connection keeps answering heartbeats in a second thread meanwhile (session.keep_alive takes a stop condition). install.sh builds the native core into the app's venv when Rust is installed (in a temporary build folder), and says so when it is not. Tests: the fake console's stream side (tests/fake_stream.py), tests/test_stream.py, and Test stream in the window test.

Changelog: 12-10-2026 22:12:19

Iteration 2 begins: the native core, a Rust crate in native/ with a Python module (bc250_remote_play_core, built with maturin). It has the stream layer's base, ported from chiaki-ng: the stream crypto (key stream and GMAC with its key refresh), the ECDH key exchange on secp256k1, and the Takion packets (GMACs, key positions, the handshake messages, data and acknowledgements, the video and audio headers of Takion 7, 9 and 12, congestion). Every part equals vectors printed by chiaki-ng's own C code (tests/takion_vectors.json), in the Rust tests and from Python (tests/test_native.py, skipped when the module is not built). Where chiaki-ng's audio parser would read past a short packet, the port refuses it. The app does not use the native core yet. install.sh does not copy cargo's build output (native/target); requirements-dev.txt has maturin.

Changelog: 12-10-2026 22:00:05

Release 0.1.0.

Changelog: 12-10-2026 21:48:58

First version, iteration 1 of BC250 Remote Play (the prototype): find PS4 and PS5 consoles on the network (broadcast, or one address), wake one from rest mode, register the board with it (PSN account ID and the PIN from Link Device), and start a session with Test session or put it in rest mode. The protocol is chiaki-ng's (AGPL-3.0), ported to Python: discovery, registration and its crypto, the session request and the control connection with heartbeats, the session ID and the login passcode. The key tables are generated from chiaki-ng's rpcrypt.c, and the crypto is tested against vectors printed by chiaki-ng's own C code. The registered consoles' keys are kept in ~/.config/bc250-remote-play/consoles.json (mode 0600). The video, audio and controller stream is not in this version. License AGPL-3.0 (with chiaki-ng's OpenSSL exception): the files ported from chiaki-ng are AGPL-3.0-only, the app's own files AGPL-3.0-or-later.
